# Soft Delete and Destructive Prompt Safety

## Context

The normalized custom-page tables remain part of the current schema but are now
scheduled for evidence-gated retirement by
`.todo/custom_page_document_model_consolidation`. Keep their existing lifecycle
safety until that plan removes them; do not treat them as the future page model.

Skaia currently hard-deletes records across forum, pages, store, inbox,
notifications, configuration, provisioning, auth/session, and relationship
repositories. Several parent deletes rely on PostgreSQL cascades; store
category/product deletion also deletes historical `order_items` explicitly.
Custom-page factory reset, typed section synchronization, and shadow projection
contain additional bulk/stale-row deletes.

The checked-in tenant schema contains about 65 active/dormant tables, while the
current `home` database contains 71. The extra
`page_interaction_*`/`page_interactive_sections` tables are orphaned remnants of
a retired design: current code and specs use `page_section_instances` and
`page_section_responses`. Two orphan tables already have `deleted_at` and
`deleted_by`; they must not be treated as an authoritative convention or
repurposed without the existing page cutover/retirement preflight.

The frontend already mounts `PromptContainer` and uses `customConfirm`,
`customPrompt`, and `customAlert`. Native browser dialogs remain in five places:
three `confirm()` calls in store order/reference-code/card deletion and two
`alert()` calls in upload failure handling. Several destructive actions,
including forum, store catalog, page comment/section, and notification deletion,
currently have no confirmation.

## Goal

- Make logical removal the default for every application database record that
  can be deleted, excluding user-upload/file contents and filesystem cleanup.
- Hide deleted records from normal reads, mutations, counts, search, cache, SEO,
  and realtime state while retaining enough data and attribution for authorized
  restoration and audit.
- Preserve containment correctly: deleting a category/page/thread/conversation
  hides its contained records without erasing independently deleted child state;
  restoration must not resurrect a child that was deleted separately.
- Preserve immutable financial, audit, analytics, and migration evidence by
  forbidding user-facing deletion rather than inventing a destructive path.
- Replace every browser-native `confirm`, `prompt`, `alert`, or unload prompt
  with Skaia UI, and require the shared destructive prompt before user-facing
  destructive commands.
- Ship migrations, repository/service/API changes, restore workflows, realtime
  convergence, tests, rollout controls, and durable specs as one verified policy.

## Non-Goals

- Do not soft-delete or retain the bytes of user uploads, exports, archives, or
  other files. Their existing physical deletion remains permanent, but must use
  Skaia confirmation/error UI.
- Do not promise restoration of external infrastructure destroyed during site,
  app, or provisioned-instance teardown. Retain the database lifecycle record
  and make the permanent external effect explicit.
- Do not expose deleted records through normal public APIs or add a generic
  endpoint that bypasses domain authorization.
- Do not add an automatic hard-purge job in this feature. Any future retention
  purge requires a separate policy, legal/security review, and restore drill.
- Do not revive or build against orphaned `page_interaction_*` tables.

## Coverage Contract

Before implementation, turn this grouping into a checked table/operation ledger
covering fresh migrations, every deployed tenant, and the `grengo` control
database. No table or runtime `DELETE FROM` may remain unclassified.

| Strategy | Records |
| --- | --- |
| Restorable entity tombstone | `users`, `roles`, `permissions`, `site_config`, `forum_categories`, `forum_threads`, `thread_comments`, `store_categories`, `products`, `orders`, `subscription_plans`, `subscriptions`, `user_cards`, `product_reviews`, `store_reference_codes`, `inbox_conversations`, `inbox_messages`, `notifications`, `pages`, `page_comments`, `data_sources`, `custom_sections`, `user_page_allocations`, `page_sections`, `page_items`, `page_themes`, `page_theme_tokens`, `page_section_instances`, `page_section_instance_items`, `page_section_presets`, `page_section_responses`, `app_blueprints`, `provisioned_instances`, `media_history` |
| Reversible relationship state | `role_permissions`, `user_roles`, `user_permissions`, `superuser_demotion_votes`, `cart_items`, `thread_likes`, `thread_comment_likes`, `thread_editors`, `inbox_conversation_participants`, `user_blocks`, `page_editors`, `page_likes`, `page_comment_likes`, `page_section_color_references` |
| Revoke/expire or scrub secrets | `user_sessions`, `sessions`, `email_verification_tokens`, `password_reset_tokens`, `auth_backup_codes`, `auth_credentials`, `auth_totp_secrets`, `mfa_challenge_required`; clearing a secret removes secret material while retaining only safe lifecycle metadata |
| Immutable/no delete API | `payments`, `order_items`, `user_wallet_transactions`, `store_reference_code_payouts`, `events`, `resource_views`; corrections use compensating/status records |
| Operational lifecycle/history | `page_section_quarantine` resolves rather than deletes; `page_section_response_migrations` and `page_section_shadow_runs` are retained/upserted; stale normalized rows use tombstones rather than hard deletion |
| Orphaned deployed tables | `page_interactive_sections`, `page_interaction_participation`, `page_interaction_qa_items`, `page_interaction_qa_upvotes`, `page_interaction_responses`, `page_interaction_selections`; verify usage/counts per tenant and route retirement through the existing page cutover gate |
| Control-plane lifecycle | `frappe_sites` and `frappe_clusters` use deleted/retired status plus timestamps; `grengo_api_keys` keeps `revoked_at`; `grengo_api_key_permissions` deactivate; `grengo_passcodes` scrub secret values and retain safe cleared metadata |
| Explicit exception | Filesystem uploads, incomplete upload chunks, exported archives, and temporary files may be physically removed; no application database row is hard-deleted as a side effect |

The ledger must also classify migration-only data cleanup separately from
runtime behavior. Existing guarded historical migrations may contain `DELETE`,
but new runtime code must pass a strict allowlist check.

## Behavioral Decisions

- Standard entity metadata is nullable `deleted_at TIMESTAMPTZ` and
  `deleted_by BIGINT REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE SET NULL`; lifecycle tables
  may use a more precise existing timestamp such as `revoked_at` or `resolved_at`.
- Normal reads and writes require `deleted_at IS NULL`. Deleted direct lookups
  return the same not-found response as missing records to avoid enumeration.
- Restore and trash reads use explicit repository methods and the same or
  stronger service policy as delete. Handlers never query around policy.
- Container deletion marks the container, not every descendant. Descendants are
  hidden through active-ancestor predicates; restoring the container reveals
  only descendants whose own tombstone is null.
- User deletion revokes authentication and memberships but does not erase
  attributed historical/public content. Rendering uses a safe deleted-user
  identity where needed; secrets are scrubbed, not retained for restoration.
- Relationship removal marks the existing unique row inactive. Re-adding a like,
  membership, permission, block, cart entry, editor, or color reference revives
  that row with an atomic upsert instead of creating duplicates.
- Delete and restore transitions are transactional and idempotent. The first
  delete actor/time is preserved; restore clears the tombstone and records an
  append-only audit event.
- User-facing unique values need an explicit per-index decision. Identity and
  security keys remain reserved. Reusable domain names/slugs may use partial
  unique indexes on active rows; restore returns `409` with a non-sensitive
  conflict code if a live replacement now owns the value.
- Deleting the homepage clears `landing_page_slug` transactionally. Restoring a
  page never silently makes it the homepage. Allocation counts, reply counts,
  catalog counts, badges, and analytics exclude deleted records and are
  reconciled from active data rather than blindly incremented/decremented.
- Existing `*_deleted` realtime actions remain the removal projection even
  though storage is retained. Restoration adds explicit `*_restored` actions or
  content-free resource invalidations; private deleted content is never pushed.

## Steps

1. **Freeze the deletion inventory and resolve schema drift**
   - Record every checked-in/live table, FK delete rule, unique constraint,
     repository `DELETE FROM`, HTTP delete route, bulk reset, filesystem side
     effect, cache invalidation, WS event, and frontend destructive control.
   - Compare `001_schema.sql`, incremental migrations, `home`, other tenants,
     test databases, and `internal/app/migrations`.
   - Confirm the orphaned interactive tables have no code/job clients and link
     their eventual retirement to `typed_page_sections_redesign`; do not block
     active-table soft delete on production retirement evidence.
   - Identify API methods using HTTP `DELETE` for reversible semantics such as
     unlike, unblock, unsuspend, logout, cart removal, or permission toggles so
     prompt policy follows user intent rather than HTTP method alone.

2. **Add idempotent schema foundations**
   - Add the next tenant migration and update `001_schema.sql`; add a separate
     sorted control-plane migration for `grengo`.
   - Add tombstone/lifecycle columns, actor FKs, active-read indexes, and
     relationship revive constraints from the coverage ledger.
   - Replace only the unique constraints approved for active-value reuse with
     partial unique indexes. Verify restore conflicts before dropping any
     existing constraint.
   - Add safe columns/statuses for secret scrubbing and external teardown
     lifecycle. Never retain passcodes, API keys, reset tokens, TOTP material,
     card secrets, or backup-code hashes merely to make a row restorable.
   - After all repositories are converted, add a database-level hard-delete
     guard for soft-deletable tables with an explicit migration/operator bypass;
     application connections must not be able to bypass it.

3. **Make active scope authoritative in backend reads**
   - Extend domain models only where deleted metadata is needed by authorized
     restore/admin DTOs; do not leak it into public payloads by default.
   - Audit every `SELECT`, join, count, search, list, feed, ownership check,
     update, duplicate, backfill, SEO/sitemap query, and analytics aggregation.
   - Require active ancestors for contained records and active target rows for
     mutations. Preserve historical order/payment lines when products, users,
     categories, or orders are removed.
   - Add explicit `GetIncludingDeleted`, `ListDeleted`, and `Restore` repository
     methods rather than boolean flags on ordinary reads.
   - Ensure page compatibility projection, normalized cutover readiness, shadow
     parity, interactive response reads, and typed revision checks cannot expose
     or mutate tombstoned pages/sections/items/responses.

4. **Convert mutations domain by domain**
   - Replace hard deletes in user/RBAC/auth/session, forum, store, inbox,
     notification, config/datasource/preset, page, provisioning, media history,
     and control-plane repositories.
   - Replace page factory reset and delete-all operations with one transaction
     that tombstones active rows, clears homepage state, reconciles allocations,
     emits bounded audit metadata, and remains retry-safe.
   - Replace normalized section/item/token/response synchronization deletes with
     tombstone/revive logic while preserving stable IDs, expected revisions,
     ordering uniqueness, rollback projection, and parity hashes.
   - Replace projection cleanup deletes with resolved/superseded lifecycle state
     where the record is evidence; keep immutable financial/audit rows intact.
   - For external teardown, commit a `deleting` state before work, finalize a
     retained `deleted` tombstone after success, and expose retry/failure state.

5. **Add restoration and trash workflows**
   - Add domain-owned restore endpoints such as
     `POST /{resource}/{id}/restore`, protected by service-level policy and audit.
   - Add bounded/paginated deleted-item reads for authorized owners/managers.
     Expose them through one authenticated `/trash` frontend route using the
     existing directory/table-view shell. The route groups tombstones by
     resource type and gives each group its own bounded table, empty state,
     pagination, and per-item undo controls.
   - Back the shared trash route with an aggregation service over domain-owned
     trash providers and normalized summary DTOs; it must not issue generic SQL,
     accept caller-selected authorization scopes, or bypass resource policies.
     Ordinary users see only resources they deleted or own and are permitted to
     restore. Administrators and callers with the relevant manage-others
     permission see all tombstones within each authorized resource domain.
     Do not expose bulk undo until the matching domain supplies an atomic bulk
     restore that reauthorizes every item and fails closed without leaking
     hidden tombstones.
   - Support single and bulk restore where the matching bulk delete exists.
     Surface uniqueness/parent-deleted conflicts as stable `409` codes.
   - Restore caches, counts, search visibility, page allocations, and realtime
     state only after the transaction succeeds. External teardowns are displayed
     as retained history/reprovision actions, not restorable resources.

6. **Complete realtime, cache, and observability behavior**
   - Evict active caches on delete/restore and prevent tombstoned cache fills.
   - Update forum/store/order/page/inbox/notification reducers for restore or
     authoritative refetch, including reconnect behavior.
   - Emit append-only delete/restore events with actor, resource type/id, result,
     and bulk correlation only; never log secrets or private response bodies.
   - Add metrics for delete, restore, conflict, hard-delete-guard rejection, and
     rows hidden by active scope. Alert on unexpected runtime hard-delete attempts.

7. **Standardize Skaia prompts and guard destructive controls**
   - Evolve `components/ui/Prompt.tsx` to accept structured title/body,
     destructive confirm label/variant, pending/disabled state, and optional
     typed-name confirmation for bulk/permanent operations. Preserve the
     promise API or provide a narrow `confirmDestructiveAction` wrapper.
   - Make the dialog accessible: `role="alertdialog"`/`aria-modal`, labelled
     title/description, initial focus, focus trap/restore, Escape/cancel behavior,
     and serialized requests so concurrent prompts never strand promises.
   - Replace the three store `confirm()` calls with the shared prompt and the two
     upload `alert()` calls with `customAlert` or a Skaia toast.
   - Add confirmation to user-facing record deletion: forum
     category/thread/comment; store category/product/order/reference code/card;
     page/page section/item/comment/interactive response; data source/preset;
     conversation/message; notification/clear-all; role/user; allocation;
     provisioning/site/export; and every bulk reset/clear action.
   - Keep high-frequency reversible intent such as unlike, cart removal,
     unblock, unsuspend, logout/session lock, and role/permission toggles out of
     delete confirmation unless the UX labels the action as destructive. They
     still use the backend lifecycle strategy assigned in the ledger.
   - Update old irreversible copy such as "cannot be undone" when restoration is
     actually available. Permanent upload/file/external teardown copy remains
     explicit.
   - Add a source-level CI check forbidding `window.confirm`, `confirm`,
     `window.prompt`, `prompt`, `window.alert`, `alert`, `beforeunload`, and
     `onbeforeunload` outside the shared test harness.

8. **Roll out in guarded releases**
   - Release additive schema first, then active-read predicates, then soft-delete
     writes/restores, then the database hard-delete guard and trash UI.
   - Use a backend feature flag until every tenant passes schema and query
     readiness checks. Do not mix hard and soft delete behavior within a domain.
   - Run a production-like delete/restore drill for page with sections and
     responses, forum category with threads/comments, store category with
     products and historical orders, user attribution/auth revocation, inbox
     conversation, and bulk notification/page reset.
   - Keep rollback non-destructive: disabling new writes must never hard-delete
     tombstoned rows. Document how old binaries are prevented from running once
     the hard-delete guard is enabled.
   - Update specs only after implementation truth changes, then record commands,
     tenant preflights, and rollout evidence in the `.tip`.

## Verification

- Schema: run fresh tenant and `grengo` migrations twice; upgrade populated test
  schemas; confirm every classified table has its required lifecycle columns,
  indexes, constraints, and hard-delete protection.
- Backend: run
  `env GOCACHE=/tmp/skaia-go-cache GOTMPDIR=/tmp JWT_SECRET=test-secret go test ./...`
  from `backend`, plus root Go tests for control-plane repository changes.
- Repository matrix: for every restorable entity prove create -> visible ->
  delete -> hidden from direct/list/search/count/update -> retained in SQL ->
  restore -> visible, including separate child tombstones and active-name
  conflicts.
- Security: prove ordinary callers cannot include/restore deleted records,
  deleted auth cannot log in, secrets are scrubbed, public 404 behavior does not
  reveal tombstones, and immutable ledger/audit rows cannot be deleted.
- Realtime/cache: prove delete and restore converge across two clients and after
  reconnect without leaking deleted content or serving stale cache/SEO results.
- Frontend: add Prompt accessibility/queue/cancel/confirm tests and focused
  destructive-handler tests proving cancel sends no request and confirm sends
  exactly one request.
- Trash: prove `/trash` renders resource-grouped directory tables, ordinary
  users can list/undo only their own authorized tombstones, resource managers
  can list/undo all tombstones only in domains they manage, pagination is
  bounded per group, and mixed unauthorized bulk restores fail without
  revealing record existence.
- Static gates:
  `rg -n "\\b(window\\.)?(confirm|prompt|alert)\\s*\\(|onbeforeunload|beforeunload" frontend/src`
  must return no application hits; runtime `DELETE FROM` must match only the
  reviewed migration/test/file exception allowlist.
- Build/lint: run frontend tests, lint, TypeScript build, and production build.
- Docs: update `.specs/backend_spec`, `.specs/frontend_spec`,
  `.specs/migrations_spec`, `.specs/realtime_wss_spec`,
  `.specs/route_resolution_spec`, page interactive/compatibility specs, and
  control-plane/infrastructure specs with verified final contracts.

